# Numerosity, TMS and risky decision-making (ds-tmsrisk)

Combined continuous theta-burst stimulation (cTBS) and fMRI dataset for the study
**"Risk attitudes causally rely on parietal magnitude representations"**
(de Hollander, Moisa & Ruff). The experiment tests whether perturbing
numerosity-tuned right parietal cortex changes how people represent payoff
magnitudes, and whether that in turn changes their risky choices.

## Design

Every participant performed the same risky-choice task in up to three sessions:

| Session | Stimulation | Purpose |
|---------|-------------|---------|
| `ses-1` | none (`baseline`) | Numerosity-PRF mapping; screening for a targetable IPS cluster; anatomical scan |
| `ses-2` | `ips` or `vertex` | First cTBS session |
| `ses-3` | the other site | Second cTBS session |

Seventy-eight participants completed session 1. Thirty-seven of them have data
from both cTBS sessions; the paper reports 35 of these, two having been excluded
as behavioural outliers (see "Known quirks" below).
Which site was stimulated in `ses-2` versus `ses-3` was counterbalanced across
participants.

**The per-session stimulation site is stored in
`sub-<label>/sub-<label>_sessions.tsv`, column `stimulation`**, with values
`baseline` (session 1, no stimulation), `ips` (experimental site: the
individually localized numerosity-tuned right intraparietal sulcus) and `vertex`
(control site). `sessions.json` at the root of the dataset describes that column.
This is the only place in the dataset that records the stimulation assignment, so
read it rather than assuming any fixed session-to-site mapping.

## Task

On each trial participants chose between two sequentially presented payoff
options, each a cloud of dots whose numerosity was the payoff in Swiss francs.
One option was safe (p = 1.0), the other risky (p = 0.55); which of the two came
first was counterbalanced within a session. Six runs of 20 trials each give 120
trials per session. The exact payoff pairs were tailored per participant from a
pre-scan calibration block so that predicted risky-choice proportions spanned
20-80%.

`task-task_events.json` documents the columns of the `_events.tsv` files. Note
that a trial spans three rows (`stimulus 1`, `stimulus 2`, `choice`) sharing one
`trial_nr`; the payoff and probability columns are filled on the stimulus rows,
the response only on the `choice` row.

## Acquisition

Philips Achieva 3T with a 32-channel head coil (SNS-Lab, Zurich Center for
Neuroeconomics). Six T2*-weighted GR-EPI runs per session (130 volumes + 5
dummies, TR = 2286 ms, TE = 30 ms, 2.5 mm in-plane, 39 slices of 2.5 mm with
0.5 mm gap, flip angle 90 degrees, SENSE 1.5 in the left-right phase-encoding
direction). A 1 mm isotropic T1-weighted MPRAGE and a FLAIR were acquired in
session 1 only. Reversed phase-encoding EPI images for susceptibility distortion
correction are in `fmap/`.

cTBS was delivered offline, immediately before scanning, with a MagPro X100
stimulator and an MRi-B91 coil (MagVenture, Farum, Denmark): 600 pulses as
bursts of three 50 Hz pulses repeated at 5 Hz for 40 s, at 80% of the individual
active motor threshold. The IPS target came from that participant's own
session-1 numerosity-PRF map via neuronavigation; the vertex control site was
defined as the intersection of the left and right central sulci at the
interhemispheric fissure.

## Known quirks and deviations from BIDS

Validated with `bids-validator` (schema 1.2.1). The list below is exhaustive for
the errors it reports; everything else validates cleanly.

- **`_events.tsv` files have no `duration` column**, which BIDS requires, and put
  `trial_nr` before `onset` where BIDS wants `onset` and `duration` as the first
  two columns. Event durations were not logged per event; `onset` is accurate.
  Rows are one event each (`stimulus 1`, `stimulus 2`, `choice`).
- **Missing values in `_events.tsv` are empty rather than `n/a`**, contrary to the
  BIDS convention. An empty `choice` on a `choice` row means the participant did
  not respond in time.
- **`_physio.log` files** are raw Philips physiological logs, not the BIDS
  `_physio.tsv.gz` + `_physio.json` format. They are kept as acquired.
- **`sub-<label>_ses-1_desc-*.optim_space-T1w_pars.nii.gz` files sit inside
  `ses-1/func/`.** These are derived numerosity-PRF parameter maps (amplitude,
  baseline, mu, sd, r2) that predate the `derivatives/` layout; they are not raw
  data.
- `sub-10/ses-1` has 5 runs instead of 6, and `sub-10/ses-1` run 6 plus
  `sub-33/ses-1` runs 1, 3, 4 and 5 have BOLD data without an `_events.tsv`.
- `sub-32/ses-2` and `sub-72/ses-4` are empty leftover directories and are
  deliberately absent from the session tables.
- **`sub-32/` also contains a stray FreeSurfer subject tree** (`mri/`, `surf/`,
  `label/`, `scripts/`, `stats/`, `tmp/`, `touch/`, `trash/`) at the subject
  level, and `sub-09/behavior/` holds non-BIDS behavioural logs. Neither belongs
  in a raw BIDS tree.
- `sub-01/ses-1/fmap/sub-01_ses-1_dir-left_run-5_epi.json` has no corresponding
  image.
- `participants.tsv`'s `tms_subject` column is `true` for the 35 participants the
  paper analyses. It is **not** simply "has cTBS sessions": `sub-22` and `sub-49`
  completed both cTBS sessions but are flagged `false` because they were excluded
  as behavioural outliers. Use the presence of a `ses-2`/`ses-3` row in the
  session table to ask "was this participant stimulated".
- `dataset_description.json` declares `BIDSVersion` 1.0.0, has no `Authors`
  field, and its `License` field is inherited OpenfMRI boilerplate that does not
  reflect a deliberate licensing decision for this dataset.

## Derivatives and code

`derivatives/` holds fMRIPrep 20.2.3 output, FreeSurfer surfaces, GLMsingle
single-trial betas, numerosity-PRF/encoding-model fits, Bayesian decoding
posteriors, TMS targeting coordinates and the cognitive-model traces. The
analysis code that produced all of it is at https://github.com/Gilles86/tms_risk;
`notes/PROVENANCE.md` in that repository maps each published figure and statistic
onto the script and derivative that produced it.
